  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 248 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nMore abundant cloud cover associated with this mid-level feature \nThursday night will lead to warmer lows than tonight with low/mid \n40s expected across the area. As the shortwave aloft traverses to \nthe east, another surface cold front slides south across the region \nFriday morning. Better low-level CAA will accompany this post-\nfrontal airmass, resulting in temperatures that are closer to \nseasonable normals (highs in the 50s to near 60), or about 5-10 \ndegrees cooler than highs on Thursday. Behind this front, a surface \nhigh moves into the region from Canada, reinforcing northerly flow \nacross the region into the weekend. With lower humidity in place and \na nearby surface high leading to calmer winds at night, radiational \ncooling will play an important role in how cool locations get this \nweekend. Cloud cover is always a challenge to forecast, especially \n3+ days in the future, leading to some variance in forecast lows. \nRegardless, probabilities for lows <32F creep back into the area \nbeginning Friday night and stay around through early next week. \nCurrently, there is a good portion of the area that has >50% chance \nfor lows <32F sometime during this timeframe. The main takeaway is \nlocations that see sufficient clearing any nights this weekend will \nfall into the 30s, potentially below the freezing mark. Like usual, \nlow-lying sheltered locations would be the favored cold spots having \nthe best chance of falling below freezing under mostly clear and \ncalm conditions.\n\nBy early next week, a signal for slight warming returns as mid-level \nridging works into the region. As a result, long range guidance \nindicates 850m temperatures warm back into double digit territory \n(near 10C) Monday into Tuesday. That would lead to highs back into \nthe mid 60s/low 70s given complete atmospheric mixing, which would \nbe seasonably warm once again. Recent LREF IQR spreads are <10 \ndegrees early next week, indicating fairly good agreement among \nguidance. Analysis of the 25th percentile for high temperatures also \nhighlights the potential for a warming trend with values that are \nslightly above seasonal averages.",
